  front of users. The Teletype Model 33, introduced in 1963, became the canonical computer terminal
  of the era. It was comparatively cheap, it spoke the newly standardized ASCII code, and it could
  punch and read paper tape.

  The Model 33 left deep marks on everything that followed:

  - [1mThe name.[0m The abbreviation "TTY," still the Unix name for a terminal device, is simply
    "Teletype."
  - [1mLine endings.[0m Carriage return and line feed are separate control characters because moving the
    print head back to the left margin and advancing the paper were separate mechanical actions.
  - [1mThe bell.[0m The bell character rang an actual bell.
  - [1mTerse commands.[0m At ten characters per second, on a machine that printed everything in upper case
    on a roll of paper, brevity was a virtue. Unix's [38;5;153mls[39m, [38;5;153mcp[39m and [38;5;153mmv[39m, and the line-oriented editor
    [38;5;153med[39m, are artifacts of that speed.

  The operating system side of this arrangement matters just as much. Unix developed a "tty driver,"
  or line discipline, in the kernel. This code echoed characters, handled erase and kill, and
  translated Control-C into an interrupt signal. It still sits between every modern terminal
  emulator and the shell it hosts.

  [1mGlass teletypes and the video terminal[0m

  Paper was slow, loud and wasteful. From the mid-1960s, manufacturers began replacing the printing
  mechanism with a cathode-ray tube. IBM's 2260, from 1964, was an early example. The Datapoint
  3300, shipped by Computer Terminal Corporation in 1969, advertised its lineage in its name, being

